   <dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Ibn Ê¿ArabÄ«â€™s Role in Ibrahim Niasseâ€™s Thinking: From the Unity of Being to Muá¸¥ammadâ€™s Names-of-the-Father</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Philipp Valentini</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">This article examines the role of Ibn Ê¿ArabÄ« in the thought of the Senegalese Sufi master IbrÄhÄ«m Niasse (1900â€“1975), primarily through Niasseâ€™s QurÊ¾anic commentary FÄ« RiyÄá¸ al-TafsÄ«r, his metaphysical treatise Sirr al-Akbar, and an account of a mystical vision from 1932. Rather than approaching Niasse as a straightforward representative of the Akbarian doctrine later known as waá¸¥dat al-wujÅ«d, I argue that Ibn Ê¿ArabÄ« occupies several distinct positions within Niasseâ€™s metaphysical dramaturgy. He provides Niasse with a vocabulary for thinking divine self-manifestation, Muá¸¥ammadan servitude, spiritual paternity, and the retrospective disclosure of meanings latent in the Adamic cycle. Yet Niasse simultaneously displaces the Akbarian emphasis on Being by foregrounding jÅ«d, the generous act of the Real, which he situates beyond the opposition between Being and non-Being and identifies with the Aá¸¥madian reality from which the Muá¸¥ammadan form emanates.The resulting picture complicates genealogies that understand West African speculative Sufism primarily through the reception of waá¸¥dat al-wujÅ«d: Niasse inherits and transforms Akbarian metaphysics into a distinctively TijÄni account of emanation, paternity, and initiatory reintegration. The castle of the celestials, As ancient opinion has it, Yet where much what they must decide upon in secret Still reaches men; and so I conjured, but wâ€¦</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description>
  <dc:subject><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>JCRT</rdf:li><rdf:li>Journal for Cultural and Religious Theory</rdf:li><rdf:li>religion</rdf:li><rdf:li>theory</rdf:li><rdf:li>Ibn Ê¿ArabÄ«</rdf:li><rdf:li>Ibrahim Niasse</rdf:li><rdf:li>Sufism</rdf:li><rdf:li>unity of being</rdf:li><rdf:li>Names-of-the-Father</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:subject></rdf:Description>
